<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Licinius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 265–<a href="/years/325">325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Licinius I, Valerius Licinianus Licinius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Licinius was Roman emperor from 308 to 324. In 313 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> agreed the policy of religious toleration known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a>, and he went on to defeat Maximinus Daia and rule the eastern provinces. Rivalry with Constantine led to war; defeated at Adrianople and Chrysopolis, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, in 324, he surrendered and was executed in 325.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/licinius.jpg" alt="Licinius" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, found in Ephesos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Licinius,_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantine the Great, having defeated <b>Licinius</b> nearby in 324 CE, refounded Byzantium on a far larger scale and dedicated it as Constantinople in 330 CE, beginning the city's second life as an imperial capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Edict of Milan (313 CE), agreed by Constantine and <b>Licinius</b>, and Constantine's subsequent patronage of the Church transformed a persecuted sect into the empire's dominant institution, with consequences that extended across two millennia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Roman Empire the emperor Constantine, after his victory at the Milvian Bridge in 312, favoured the church; in 313 he and his co-emperor <b>Licinius</b> granted Christians freedom of worship, and in 325 he called the Council of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The outnumbered army of the Roman emperor <b>Licinius</b> routed his rival Maximinus Daia at Tzirallum, identified with modern Çorlu in Eastern Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In their second civil war, Emperor Constantine I soundly defeated his rival and former colleague <b>Licinius</b> at Adrianople in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Nicomedia, Emperor <b>Licinius</b> issued the letter agreed with Constantine granting freedom of worship to Christians and all others.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-13">13 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
